ery%2C_Rome.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t63-52cabb004ec7","title":"Edict of Nantes","lane":"history","start":1598,"end":1598,"approx":false,"note":"Religious settlement under Henry IV.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edict_of_Nantes","sourceSheets":[3638,3639],"sourceMapping":"period context","dateType":"year","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Historical setting"},{"id":"t63-181f4f8f6e73","title":"Gian Lorenzo Bernini","lane":"people","start":1598,"end":1680,"approx":false,"note":"Sculptor and architect.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gian_Lorenzo_Bernini","sourceSheets":[3638,3639],"sourceMapping":"period context","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Cultural association · not necessarily the place of creation"},{"id":"t63-355beea6585e","title":"Calling of Saint Matthew","lane":"art","start":1599,"end":1600,"approx":false,"note":"Contarelli Chapel; light and gesture connect the sacred story to contemporary life.","creators":["Caravaggio"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Calling_of_St_Matthew","sourceSheets":[3640],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=cea1e3f9e820308f7ce72a74.webp","credit":"Gleb Simonov","license":"CC0","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caravaggio_%E2%80%94_The_Calling_of_Saint_Matthew.jpg","scope":"work"},"threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Cultural association · not necessarily the place of creation","mediaType78":"visual-artwork"},{"id":"t63-0db69c5027a1","title":"Martyrdom of Saint Matthew","lane":"art","start":1599,"end":1600,"approx":false,"note":"Companion to the Calling in the Contarelli Chapel.","creators":["Caravaggio"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Martyrdom_of_Saint_Matthew","sourceSheets":[3639],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Cultural association · not necessarily the place of creation","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=7981d2d090d85d4fc12232c2.webp","credit":"Caravaggio","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caravaggio_%E2%80%94_The_Martyrdom_of_Saint_Matthew.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t63-78d94745b093","title":"Hamlet","lane":"art","start":1599,"end":1601,"approx":true,"note":"Composition dates approximate; first publication 1603.","creators":["William Shakespeare"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamlet","sourceSheets":[3640],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["britain"],"setting67":"Cultural association · not necessarily the place of creation","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t63-ffe0f5bd554d","title":"Anthony van Dyck","lane":"people","start":1599,"end":1641,"approx":false,"note":"Painter.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_van_Dyck","sourceSheets":[3638,3639],"sourceMapping":"period context","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["low"],"setting67":"Cultural association · not necessarily the place of creation"},{"id":"t63-be42cbccbe2d","title":"Diego Velázquez","lane":"people","start":1599,"end":1660,"approx":false,"note":"Spanish court painter.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diego_Vel%C3%A1zquez","sourceSheets":[3638,3639],"sourceMapping":"period context","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["iberia"],"setting67":"Cultural association · not necessarily the place of creation"},{"id":"t63-ba78f8fcfda2","title":"Francesco Borromini","lane":"people","start":1599,"end":1667,"approx":false,"note":"Architect.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francesco_Borromini","sourceSheets":[3638,3639],"sourceMapping":"period context","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Cultural association · not necessarily the place of creation"},{"id":"t63-a65bb5c803e2","title":"English East India Company chartered","lane":"history","start":1600,"end":1600,"approx":false,"note":"Royal charter from Elizabeth I.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_India_Company","sourceSheets":[3640],"sourceMapping":"period context","dateType":"year","threads67":["britain"],"setting67":"Historical setting"},{"id":"t63-312328941f3e","title":"Crucifixion of Saint Peter","lane":"art","start":1600,"end":1601,"approx":false,"note":"Cerasi Chapel, Santa Maria del Popolo, Rome.","creators":["Caravaggio"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crucifixion_of_Saint_Peter_(Caravaggio)","sourceSheets":[3639],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Cultural association · not necessarily the place of creation","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=943363535122fb4e4fc7c0fc.webp","credit":"Caravaggio","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crucifixion_of_Saint_Peter-Caravaggio_%28c.1600%29.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t63-ff8959caa6b8","title":"Claude Lorrain","lane":"people","start":1600,"end":1682,"approx":true,"note":"Landscape painter; birth approximate.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Lorrain","sourceSheets":[3640],"sourceMapping":"period context","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["shared"],"setting67":"Cross-regional / setting not yet specified"},{"id":"t69-01d218155f42","title":"Dutch genres and the art market","lane":"art","start":1600,"end":1700,"approx":true,"note":"Portraits, landscapes, interiors and still lifes served varied buyers in the Dutch Republic. A less court-centred market does not mean an absence of religious or political meaning.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Dutch%20genres%20and%20the%20art%20market","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["low"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t69-89ca15069eaa","title":"Tenebrism and selective illumination","lane":"art","start":1600,"end":1700,"approx":true,"note":"Strong dark fields and concentrated light heighten dramatic focus. Tenebrism is a more specific lighting effect than chiaroscuro generally.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Tenebrism%20and%20selective%20illumination","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t69-3ac77ec15ea6","title":"Vanitas and still-life symbolism","lane":"art","start":1600,"end":1700,"approx":true,"note":"Skulls, extinguished candles and timepieces can evoke mortality and transience. Read symbols in the context of the specific work instead of assuming every object has one meaning.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Vanitas%20and%20still-life%20symbolism","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["low"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t69-fd5546c30a95","title":"Absolutism, court ritual and architecture","lane":"history","start":1600,"end":1750,"approx":true,"note":"Palaces and gardens can organise access, ceremony and political visibility. Absolutism describes claims and structures of power, not the absence of every constraint.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Absolutism%2C%20court%20ritual%20and%20architecture","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept"},{"id":"t63-52e79589e474","title":"Baroque","lane":"art","start":1600,"end":1750,"approx":true,"note":"Broad European period; regional and medium-specific chronologies differ.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baroque","sourceSheets":[3640],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["shared"],"setting67":"Cross-regional / setting not yet specified","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t69-085ee7a7f524","title":"Baroque movement and theatrical space","lane":"architecture","start":1600,"end":1750,"approx":true,"note":"Curved forms, directed light and coordinated painting, sculpture and architecture can guide a visitor's experience. Compare Borromini's spatial geometry with Bernini's theatrical ensembles.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Baroque%20movement%20and%20theatrical%20space","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept"},{"id":"t69-4cbe4c257468","title":"Quadratura and illusionistic ceilings","lane":"art","start":1600,"end":1750,"approx":true,"note":"Painted architecture can seem to extend actual space; perspective must be coordinated with the viewer's position. Distinguish illusionistic construction from simple decorative pattern.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Quadratura%20and%20illusionistic%20ceilings","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t63-c3c11496d84f","title":"Conversion on the Way to Damascus","lane":"art","start":1601,"end":1601,"approx":false,"note":"Second Cerasi Chapel version; distinguish the first commission's panel.","creators":["Caravaggio"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conversion_on_the_Way_to_Damascus","sourceSheets":[3639],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=dd0e5878a143320f9a944f04.webp","credit":"Caravaggio","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conversion_on_the_Way_to_Damascus-Caravaggio_(c.1600-1).jpg","scope":"work"},"threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Cultural association · not necessarily the place of creation","mediaType78":"visual-artwork"},{"id":"t63-e7528d4b1d8e","title":"Supper at Emmaus: London version","lane":"art","start":1601,"end":1601,"approx":false,"note":"Distinguish from the later Milan version of 1606.","creators":["Caravaggio"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supper_at_Emmaus_(Caravaggio,_London)","sourceSheets":[3640],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=ff2f12adff42ec429b1c7e2a.webp","credit":"Caravaggio","license":"Public domain artwork reproduction","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Supper_at_Emmaus-Caravaggio_%281601%29.jpg","scope":"Full painting; version verified against the named collection."},"threads67":["britain"],"setting67":"Historical setting","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t63-a02ef3cc906b","title":"Alonso Cano","lane":"people","start":1601,"end":1667,"approx":false,"note":"Painter, sculptor and architect.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedi